        "What can we do about this? Another approach is to have a doubly linked-list. Then, we can insert a new element without requiring a dummy slot. But, that adds another `8`-bytes to everything. Also, it flushes out the L1d cache; the whole thing sounds expensive. So, we drop this approach. We are back to square one. We go back to the drawing board with our simplified view of the `unordered_set` without the dummy node. What did we gain? Since, we decided to drop the dummy node, that one less indirection on `find()` and `insert()`. We do we stand to lose? Iteration now take $O(\\text{size} + \\text{capacity})$. \n",
