      "source": [
        "So, what did we lose, when we did this. Now, when the table resizes, we need to recompute the hash, which is potentially expensive, because we have to call the `hash` function on every element in the table. Is that really expensive? It depends on the type `T`. If its an `int` or a `double` computing a hash function is practically free. If its a big complicated type, yes it would be expensive. Its a trade-off. Now, when we run `find()`, we might have to run `operator==()` more often. The question now is, is equality comparison expensive? It depends on the type. If you're comparing `string`s, then it is expensive. But, if you are comparing `int`s or `double`s, equality is cheap to compute. On the plus side, users can get this back - you can put in a type `T` that defines a `hash(T)` member-function and can hash its own value. So, we didn't technically lose any power, we just lost some convenience. Again, we gained some bytes of memory. \n",
